{"archetypes":[{"name":"fd-quiet-routing","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"The diff adds a new chat-print loop in render-final-summary.sh that routes to FD 3 vs FD 1 based on LARCH_QUIET_PID, moves larch_quiet_init earlier (before OUTCOME validation), and relies on lib-quiet.sh being sourced correctly; static correctness reviewers may miss bash FD management subtleties.","prompt_body":"Examine every FD 3 / FD 1 write path introduced or modified in this diff: the new chat-print loop in skills/design/scripts/render-final-summary.sh (post phase), the pre-existing loop in skills/implement/scripts/write-final-report.sh, and the larch_quiet_init placement relative to early-exit paths. Check whether LARCH_QUIET_PID is correctly set before the loop in all callers and in subshell/subprocess contexts. Verify that moving `source lib-quiet.sh` and `larch_quiet_init` above the `[ -n \"$OUTCOME\" ]` guard cannot cause FD leaks or duplicate-descriptor issues on the usage-exit paths. Confirm that a post-phase run where the pre-phase set up FD 3 does not double-write or lose output when the process ID check passes. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"fallback-body-schema","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"Both compose_self_fallback implementations must exactly mirror the renderer's conditional bullet schema (Outcome only for bailed*/stalled/cancelled-*/failed-*; PR omitted when N/A for implement; PR and Code review always omitted for design); plan-fidelity will check plan alignment but a focused pass on the field-ordering and conditional logic in the self-composed bodies is needed.","prompt_body":"Compare the compose_self_fallback function in skills/implement/scripts/write-final-report.sh and in skills/design/scripts/render-final-summary.sh against the renderer's documented schema from scripts/render-run-summary.sh. Check field ordering (Outcome immediately after title, before Mode/Path/Duration), the conditions under which the Outcome bullet is omitted versus present, the conditional PR bullet for implement (omitted when PR_NUMBER is 0 or empty), and the unconditional omission of PR and Code-review bullets for design. Also verify that notes_tmp content is appended correctly after the sentinel in the implement Stage 2 fallback. Confirm the sentinel line `<!-- larch:run-summary v=1 -->` appears exactly once and in the right position in each path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"sentinel-state-gate","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"The .step17-printed sentinel is written only when write-final-report.sh succeeds AND the cost line is present in summary-final.md, then Step 18 checks for sentinel absence to decide whether to pass --print-stdout; the two-condition write creates a non-obvious state where a partial success leaves no sentinel and Step 18 double-prints.","prompt_body":"Trace all code paths that write or check $IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/.step17-printed in skills/implement/SKILL.md (Step 17 Bash block and Step 18 _wfr_printed logic). Verify that the sentinel-write condition (script success AND cost line present) is consistent with the Step 18 _wfr_printed flag's condition. Identify scenarios where the sentinel is absent but the summary was already printed (e.g., write-final-report.sh succeeded but rendered a cost-unavailable body with no `- **Cost**:` line), and whether Step 18 would then print the summary a second time to chat. Check whether `touch $IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/.step17-printed 2>/dev/null || true` in Step 18 could write the sentinel on a bail path where Step 17 never ran, permanently suppressing future Step 18 prints on resume. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"token-zero-detection","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"render-final-summary.sh changed the _cost_unavailable trigger from 'sum_b==0 AND stderr_nonempty' to 'sum_b==0 AND total_t==0', silently altering behavior for runs where bucket counts are zero but per-vendor totals are nonzero; this new elif branch passes only totals (not buckets) to the renderer which may conflict with per-bucket pricing logic.","prompt_body":"In skills/design/scripts/render-final-summary.sh, examine the new _cost_unavailable logic: the condition `if [ \"$sum_b\" -eq 0 ] && [ \"$total_t\" -eq 0 ]` versus the old `if [ \"$sum_b\" -eq 0 ] && [ \"$stderr_nonempty\" = true ]`, and the new elif branch that passes only claude/codex/cursor totals (not per-bucket) when sum_b==0 but total_t>0. Check whether the renderer's token-cost.sh handles the case where per-bucket args are all zero but `--claude-tokens N` is nonzero — specifically whether blended-rate warnings are suppressed when only totals are provided. Also check write-final-report.sh's TOKEN_DATA_AVAILABLE flag: the condition `sum_b != 0 || total_t != 0` vs. the design script's condition; verify both scripts treat the same states identically for consistency. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."]}
